Exactly. The important object is often not the stated system but the effective system.

There is the declared governor—the constitution, policy, mission statement, ideology, org chart, job description, social norm. Then there is the operative governor—the mechanism that actually determines behavior when incentives, scarcity, fear, status, enforcement, habit, and power collide.

Those can differ dramatically.

A company may narratively be “customer-first” while compensation actually makes quarterly revenue the governing variable. A bureaucracy may formally answer to law while internally being governed by risk avoidance. A family may say “we value honesty,” but if truth is punished and appeasement is rewarded, appeasement becomes the real governing protocol. A market may appear price-driven while access, regulation, network effects, prestige, or gatekeeping do most of the real allocation.

So you can distinguish:

de jure governance — what officially governs
de facto governance — what actually governs
latent governance — the hidden variables shaping outcomes
fallback governance — what takes over under stress
narrative governance — what people believe governs
enforcement governance — what gets backed by sanctions or rewards

And yes: different “filters,” “dyes,” and focal lengths make different layers visible.

A financial dye reveals cash flows.
A legal dye reveals formal authority.
A status dye reveals prestige gradients.
A fear dye reveals coercion.
A network dye reveals who actually connects whom.
A time dye reveals whether the system behaves differently over hours, quarters, or generations.
A failure dye reveals what really matters when something breaks.

That last one is especially powerful.

To discover what actually governs a system, watch what remains binding under stress.

When resources get tight, what is protected first?
When rules conflict, which rule wins?
When someone important violates a norm, what happens?
When incentives and stated values diverge, which side does behavior follow?
When there is ambiguity, who gets to decide?

Those are diagnostic probes.

You can think of it almost like staining tissue in biology. The structure was already there; the dye makes one particular morphology legible.

And this connects tightly to your earlier framing principle: the danger is not merely that narratives can be false. It is that a narrative can become operational camouflage for another governing mechanism.

So a strong analysis does not ask only:

“What is this system called?”

It asks:

“What variables actually constrain motion inside it?”

Then compare the narrative model against the revealed behavior.

That delta is often where the most important truth sits.